# Linear automation (ground truth)

**In Review** and **Done** are **automation-owned**, not agent-written. **In
Progress** is agent-writable **once**, at approved `/sdd-to-tdd` execution
start (`linear-resolver` START); GitHub `On PR open/update → In Progress` is
the backup. Agents post START/resolution comments and Git/PR artifacts;
Linear's team automations move issues through In Review and Done. See
[Linear GitHub docs — Workflow automation](https://linear.app/docs/github#workflow-automation).

## Cycle

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  SDD["/sdd-to-tdd execution"]
  Start["linear-resolver START"]
  Loop["Red Green Refactor"]
  LR["linear-resolver CLOSE-OUT comment"]
  Commit["/commit PASS"]
  Push["/push optional PR arg"]
  PR["resolved PR"]
  PromoCheck{"base == default branch?"}
  Prep["inject Fixes trailers"]
  OpMerge["Operator merge in GitHub"]
  SDD --> Start
  Start --> IP["In Progress"]
  IP --> Loop
  Loop --> LR
  LR -->|"comment automation"| IR1["In Review optional"]
  LR --> Commit
  Commit -->|"local commit Fixes REAZED-###"| Local["Commit on branch"]
  Local --> Push
  Push -->|"git push + PR open/update"| IPBackup["In Progress backup via PR"]
  Push --> PR
  PR --> PromoCheck
  PromoCheck -->|"yes"| Prep
  PromoCheck -->|"no"| Skip["skip prep — trailers aggregate later"]
  Prep -->|"agent requests PR review"| IR2["In Review via PR activity"]
  Skip -->|"agent requests PR review"| IR2
  IR2 --> Ready["PR ready to merge"]
  Ready --> OpMerge
  OpMerge --> Done["Done via PR merge automation"]
```

`/sdd-to-tdd` execution (after plan approval) is what first moves the **invoked
issue** to **In Progress** via `linear-resolver` START — Plan Mode production
does not write Linear. `/commit` stays entirely Linear-free (local commit only,
never pushes). A single `/push` may re-fire **In Progress** via PR open/update
as backup (typically a no-op), preps the promotion close-out whenever the PR it
resolves targets the default branch, and may **request PR review**
(`gh pr edit --add-reviewer`) to fire **In Review** — it never calls
`gh pr merge` and never calls Linear MCP. `/push` creates PRs as **drafts**
(GitHub Actions jobs are gated on `pull_request.draft == false`) and never
runs `gh pr ready`, so on a fresh PR **In Review** fires only once the
**operator** readies the draft; **In Progress** still fires immediately via
the `Draft PR open` automation. The operator is the only actor
that merges a PR; that merge event is what Linear's `On PR merge → Done`
automation reacts to.

## GitHub PR automations

**Configured in Linear Settings → Team → Workflows & automations → Pull request
and commit automations** (per team):

| Event | Linear transition |
|-------|-------------------|
| Draft PR open | → In Progress |
| PR open | → In Progress |
| PR review request or activity | → In Review |
| PR ready for merge | → No action |
| **PR merge** | **→ Done** (closing-linked PR into **`staging` or the default branch**) |

`On PR merge → Done` is a **team-level automation on the PR-merge event**. It
fires only for a PR that is *closing-linked* to the issue — a
`Fixes`/`Closes`/`Resolves` + issue ID that Linear can see on **that specific
PR** (title, body, or its own not-previously-merged commits). A merge into
**`staging` or the default branch** both count. Feature PRs base to `staging`
and close at that merge; the default-branch merge is the `staging` promotion
PR. See
[.cursor/rules/staging-accumulator.mdc](.cursor/rules/staging-accumulator.mdc).

## Comment / message automations

The team may also configure **comment or message automations** in Linear
Settings → Team → Workflows & automations (e.g. transition to In Review when a
close-out resolution comment is posted). These are operator-maintained; agents
do not replicate them with `save_issue`.

The structured close-out comment from `linear-resolver` uses stable section
headers (`Root cause:`, `Spec updated:`, `Verification:`, etc.) so team
automations can match them reliably. If the operator changes the trigger
pattern, update the **Maintenance** note below — never reintroduce agent state
writes as a substitute.

The START comment **is** a **plan digest** — a single, bounded summary, never
the plan file itself. Its labels (`Mode:`, `Owning spec:`, `Criteria:`,
`Approval gates:`, `Infra:`, `Full plan:`, `Problem:`, `Approach:`,
`Out-of-scope findings:`) are deliberately disjoint from those close-out
headers. Do **not** add a comment automation that matches the digest or the
`Work started:` line.

## The accumulator-branch gap

This gap applies to **direct commits on `staging`** (the local `/dispatch`
lane), not to feature PRs. Per Linear's GitHub re-merge behavior, a branch that
already merged other PRs does **not** resurface those PRs' links when it is
later merged again. A `Fixes REAZED-###` trailer in a commit that landed **directly
on `staging`** therefore does **not**, by itself, close the issue when
`staging` is later promoted to the default branch — the *promotion PR itself*
must carry the closing words. `/push` run from `staging` (or pinned with
`/push <promotion-PR-URL>` if auto-discovery would be ambiguous) aggregates
every closing trailer across that promotion PR's commits and injects them into
the PR body — it never merges; the operator merges once checks are green.

A **feature PR** into `staging` is a different path: its own commits were not
previously merged, so the closing trailer on those commits (and `/commit`'s
`Fixes REAZED-###`) is visible on that PR. `On PR merge → Done` fires at the
staging merge. `/push` does not promotion-prep that PR (base is not the
default branch).

Before a promotion PR exists, GitHub PR automations (`On PR open`, `On PR review
activity`) will not fire for *direct* `staging` commits. START has already
moved the invoked issue to In Progress at execution start. The close-out
resolution comment (and any team comment/message automation on it) plus
`/push` review-request (on a PR the operator has readied) provide In Review
visibility until the operator merges.

## Workflow state writers

**START carve-out (In Progress only).** `linear-resolver` START — delegated as
the first `/sdd-to-tdd` *execution* action after plan approval — may call
`save_issue` to set **In Progress**, and only **forward** from Backlog or Todo,
on the **invoked issue only** (not `relatedTo` / parent / children). FEATURE
mode runs START only when the plan's `linear_issue` is set; free-text `bug:`
with no tracked issue skips START. START posts a single bounded `Work
started:` summary comment (Problem/Approach/Out-of-scope findings; bounded to
`START_SUMMARY_MAX_CHARS` — the plan file itself is never posted to Linear,
in any form); that is **not** a workflow-state write, so it
runs even where the state table skips (In Review, terminal). START must not
call `save_document`. `save_comment` (and leftover `save_document` if ever
used) is scanned by `linear-spawn-guard`; an oversized `save_comment` is
denied by `linear-comment-size-guard`
([.cursor/hooks/lib/linear-comment-size-policy.mjs](.cursor/hooks/lib/linear-comment-size-policy.mjs)).
It never opens a
Cloud Agent spawn door: `save_issue.assignee` (any value, including `null`),
`save_issue.delegate`, or an `@Cursor` mention in a comment, title,
description, document, or `patch` op. Per
[Cursor's Linear docs](https://cursor.com/docs/integrations/linear), those
are what spawn a Cloud Agent. The local `linear-spawn-guard` hook denies
them on `beforeMCPExecution`; that event is unavailable in cloud
agents, so a cloud agent with Linear MCP access is still restrained by
this prose. It never regresses In Review → In Progress, and
never reopens Done / Canceled / Duplicate. Already In Progress is a no-op.
START MCP failure is visibility-only — the TDD loop continues. Plan Mode
production does not write Linear.

**CLOSE-OUT never writes workflow state.** `linear-resolver` CLOSE-OUT posts
the structured resolution comment only (`save_comment`). Agents and commands
never call `save_issue` to set In Review, In Progress, or Done during fix
close-out.

- **In Progress** — primary: START at approved `/sdd-to-tdd` execution.
  Backup: `On PR open/update` when `/push` publishes the branch (typically a
  no-op if START already moved it).
- **In Review** — GitHub PR review activity when `/push` requests review on
  an already-ready PR, when the **operator** readies `/push`'s draft
  (`gh pr ready`), or when the operator reviews directly; team
  comment/message automation on the close-out comment when configured. Never
  agent-written via `save_issue`.
- **Done** — `On PR merge → Done` when a closing-linked PR merges into
  **`staging` or the default branch**. A feature PR's own
  not-previously-merged commits carry `/commit`'s `Fixes REAZED-###` trailer, so
  the staging merge closes the issue. Direct commits on `staging` still need
  the promotion PR to carry the aggregated closing line (`/push` injects it
  when base is the default branch). The **operator** performs that merge in
  the GitHub UI. Never agent-written.

If an In Review or Done automation doesn't fire (mislinked PR, integration
hiccup), that is an **operator fallback in the Linear UI** — never something
an agent compensates for with a direct `save_issue` state write, and never
something delegated to `linear-resolver` as a workaround. START is not a
fallback for a missed PR-open In Progress; it is the primary In Progress write
at TDD start.

**GROOM mode** (`/triage`) is narrowly scoped, not a general exception: an
operator-approved grooming batch may move an issue **Backlog ↔ Todo**, or to
**Canceled** or **Duplicate** (each with a linking comment), via `save_issue`
— both are terminal, GROOM-settable states, not automation-owned (unlike In
Review/Done below). A **prunable-class sweep batch** (see
`docs/findings/README.md` Issue-filing policy) may move several issues to
Canceled under one operator confirmation on the named batch instead of
per-issue confirmation — still one linking comment per issue. GROOM must
**never** target **In Progress, In Review, or Done**. In Review and Done only
ever change via the GitHub PR automations above. In Progress is START-writable
from `/sdd-to-tdd` execution only (PR open remains the backup) — not by GROOM.

## Issue-property conventions (milestone, estimate, filing policy)

The **filing floor, attach-over-create ladder, per-run issue cap, ledger TTL,
milestone convention** (live Platform names: `M8 — Launch Acceptance
(Payment 3)` / `M6–M7 — UAT Complete Production Deployment` /
`Post-launch hardening` — never invent `Launch-blocking`), and
**estimate crosswalk** live in `docs/findings/README.md` — the single policy
`linear-resolver`, `/sdd-to-tdd` STEP 4C, `/triage`, and `/audit` PART 8 all
cite. This file governs *workflow state* (In Progress via START + PR-open
backup; In Review/Done via automations; and the GROOM-settable states above);
`docs/findings/README.md` governs *which issues get created at all* and the
non-workflow properties (priority, milestone, estimate, labels) set on them.
Update that file, not this one, if the filing policy or property conventions
change.

## Operator merge checklist

The only human-performed step in this cycle is merging in the GitHub UI.
Two paths:

**Feature PR** (head `sdd/REAZED-###` or any non-`staging` feature branch → base
`staging`) — lighter; `/push` does not promotion-prep it:

1. Confirm `/push` opened the PR as `<head> → staging` and requested review
   (or reported the single-operator caveat).
2. Confirm required checks are green (`/push` reports this advisorily; verify
   in the GitHub UI before merging).
3. Merge the feature PR in the GitHub UI. The PR's own `Fixes REAZED-###` trailer
   is what Linear sees; the issue should move to **Done** at this merge.
4. If Done does not fire (~1 minute), check the closing link on **that** PR.

**Promotion PR** (head `staging` → default branch) — needed for leftover
**direct commits** on `staging`:

1. Run `/push` from `staging` (or `/push <promotion-PR-URL>` if discovery is
   ambiguous) — confirm the `## Linear close-out` block /
   `Fixes REAZED-###[, REAZED-###, ...]` line is present on **that** PR (not just
   buried in staging commits).
2. Confirm required checks are green (`/push` reports this advisorily; verify
   in the GitHub UI before merging).
3. Merge the promotion PR in the GitHub UI.
4. Verify the linked issue(s) that were only on direct `staging` commits move
   to **Done** in Linear (~1 minute); if not, check the closing link on the
   merged promotion PR itself.

If an In Review or Done automation misfires on either path, fix the state
**in the Linear UI yourself** — never via `linear-resolver` CLOSE-OUT/GROOM
or any agent compensating `save_issue` call. START is the only agent In
Progress write, and only at `/sdd-to-tdd` execution start — not a
merge-fallback. Never `gh pr merge`.

## Maintenance

If the operator changes the team's Workflows & Automations settings, update
this file:

- The **GitHub PR table** above — `/commit`, `/push`, and `linear-resolver`
  assume `On PR merge → Done` is configured as stated. `On PR open → In
  Progress` is backup; START is the primary In Progress write.
- Any **comment/message automation trigger** the team relies on for In Review
  on close-out (document the pattern here when known; e.g. comment containing
  `Verification:`).
- Do **not** add a Linear automation that moves to In Review or Done from the
  START `Work started:` summary comment — that header and its
  labels (`Mode:`, `Owning spec:`, `Criteria:`, `Approval gates:`, `Infra:`,
  `Full plan:`, `Problem:`, `Approach:`, `Out-of-scope findings:`) are an
  audit trail only. Close-out automations must keep matching close-out
  headers (`Verification:`, `Root cause:`, …), never the START summary.
